      clientImplication: "CSCOs and Sales Directors face a structural decision: redesign urban RTM now with first-mover advantage, or wait and face forced redesign at higher cost as distributor economics deteriorate. The decision involves redesigning coverage models, stockist economics, channel P&L tracking, and direct-to-retailer investment. This is a 12-24 month transformation, not a quick fix.",
      thoucentriqAngle: "Classic S&D transformation engagement: (1) Coverage Model Diagnostic — assess current distributor economics, outlet-level profitability, and channel shift patterns in 3-4 representative cities. (2) RTM Architecture Design — design the future model: which outlets go direct, which stay through stockist, which channels get dedicated GTM teams. (3) Implementation Support — pilot design, distributor transition management, technology enablement (DMS upgrade, route optimization).",
      pitchAnchor: "We have been tracking distributor EBITDA trends across India FMCG for three quarters, and the data is consistent: in urban markets, the traditional stockist model is losing its economic rationale. The high-value outlets — premium supermarkets, large-format modern trade, high-footfall kirana stores — are increasingly served by quick-commerce platforms at unit economics the distributor cannot match. The companies that will win the next decade are the ones redesigning their urban RTM right now, not after the model has fully broken. We have built a coverage model diagnostic that tells you exactly where the model is breaking and what the future architecture should look like.",
      provocQuestion: "What percentage of your top 1,000 revenue-generating outlets in Mumbai or Delhi are now also served by at least one quick-commerce platform, and what has happened to your average order value in those outlets over the last 4 quarters?",
      povParagraph: "The India FMCG route-to-market model that drove three decades of growth is at a structural inflection point. The distributor/stockist chain was built for a world where the only way to reach 12 million kirana stores was through a layered intermediary network. Quick-commerce has demonstrated that urban consumers will pay a premium for 10-minute delivery, and in doing so, it has fundamentally changed the economics of the traditional high-value outlet. The companies that recognize this as a structural shift — not a temporary disruption — and redesign their urban coverage models now will have a significant first-mover advantage. The ones that wait will find their distributors renegotiating margins, their best outlet relationships migrating to platforms, and their RTM cost structure built for a channel mix that no longer exists.",
      version: "1.0",
      nextReview: "2025-07-01",
