      whyItMatters: "The technology stack of a client company is the single best predictor of their SC transformation readiness. Companies running fragmented ERP landscapes (multiple regional ERPs, no integrated data layer) cannot deploy advanced planning, demand sensing, or GenAI — regardless of budget or intent. Understanding the technology landscape before an engagement design is not optional; it is the diagnostic lens through which every other recommendation is filtered.",
      industryLandscape: "Indian FMCG technology maturity follows a predictable hierarchy: Mobile order-taking (universal A-level); DMS (widespread D-A range); Core ERP (mostly SAP in large companies, fragmented in mid-tier); Advanced planning (rare); GenAI (deployed use cases extremely rare).\n\nGlobal leaders (P&G, Unilever, Nestlé) are 5-8 years ahead of the India average on planning and intelligence layers. India leaders (HUL, ITC) are actively investing but 2-4 years behind on GenAI and advanced analytics. Mid-tier India players (Marico, Dabur, Emami) have the widest gap — and the biggest consulting opportunity.",
      failureModes: "**ERP Fragmentation from M&A:** Companies formed through acquisition often run 3-5 ERPs across geographies and business units. This prevents integrated planning and makes any GenAI deployment impossible.\n\n**Data Lake Without Data Quality:** Companies building data lakes without investing in master data management are creating expensive noise repositories rather than intelligence infrastructure.\n\n**Tool Proliferation Without Integration:** Excel-based S&OP on top of SAP with a separate DMS and an unconnected analytics platform is a common failure mode — multiple tools, no single version of truth.\n\n**GenAI Pilots Without Foundation:** Running AI pilots on bad data produces confident wrong answers. The pilot succeeds on demo day and fails in production.",
