      whyItMatters: "GenAI's value in FMCG is not uniformly distributed — some use cases have high ROI and low implementation risk; others are technically impressive but operationally marginal. This catalog exists to give consultants a structured view of the use case landscape so they can help clients prioritize investment in high-value, implementable use cases rather than impressive pilots.",
      industryLandscape: "Current deployment patterns by tier:\n\n**Global Leaders (P&G, Unilever, Nestlé):** 10-50+ deployed use cases across demand sensing, procurement, R&D, marketing, and SC. Full data infrastructure prerequisite met.\n\n**India Leaders (HUL, ITC):** 3-10 deployed use cases; demand forecasting and consumer insights most common. Benefiting from parent company global infrastructure.\n\n**India Mid-Tier (Dabur, Marico, Godrej CP):** 0-3 pilots; mostly consumer insights and marketing. No deployed SC use cases. Data infrastructure gap is the binding constraint.",
      failureModes: "**Starting with High-Complexity Use Cases:** Companies beginning GenAI journeys with demand sensing or formulation AI fail because these require the most data infrastructure. Starting with document processing or summarization produces quick wins that fund the harder work.\n\n**Confusing Pilot Success with Production Readiness:** A GenAI pilot that works on curated data in a controlled environment is not production-ready. The gap between pilot and production is where most Indian FMCG GenAI initiatives stall.\n\n**No Change Management for AI Outputs:** Demand planners who don't trust AI-generated forecasts will override them consistently, eliminating the value entirely. Change management is not optional.",
